   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to including certain\nspent fuel rods within the definition of real property",
    "basePrintNo": "S7671",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7671-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §102, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7671\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to including\ncertain spent fuel rods within the definition of real property\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to include certain spent fuel rods in\nwithin the definition of real property.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Paragraph (j) of subdivision 12 of section 102 of the Real\nProperty Tax Law, as added by chapter 385 of the laws of 2020, is\namended to add spent fuel rods to the already existing spent fuel pools\nand dry cask storage systems.\n\nSection 2. Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nNot applicable at this time.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2020, similar legislation was signed into law adding spent fuel pools\nand dry cast storage systems into the definition of real property. This\nbill also adds spent fuel rods.\n\nCurrently, spent nuclear fuel rods are being housed at the now closed\nIndian Point Energy Center in Buchanan, New York, and at other nuclear\npower plants across the state and country. In the 95th Assembly\nDistrict, rods came from Con Edison, the New York Power Authority Gener-\nating Facilities, and most recently, from the power company, Energy. For\nmany years, the Federal Government has promised a site will be provided\nfor the storage of these rods. However, this site has still not been\nconstructed, and may not be for many more years to come.\n\nSince the federal government has failed to locate a permanent home for\nthese spent fuel rods, places like the Indian Point have become the\nstorage site for this nuclear waste.\n\nTo help you better understand, Indian Point's website previously clari-\nfied the way they store spent nuclear fuel. \"Water serves as a natural\nand one of the most effective - barriers to radiation. This is one other\nreason that used, or spent, fuel is stored in pools.  Rods containing\nuranium fuel pellets are bundled together to form one fuel assembly.\nEach assembly is about 13 feet high and 8 inches square. After it spends\nabout four years in the reactor giving off heat, a fuel assembly is\nspent and ready to be stored in water in the spent fuel pool.\"\n\nAt an August 18th, 2021, Government to Government meeting with the\nNuclear Regulatory Committee (NRC), it was confirmed and made very clear\nthat spent fuel rods are owned by the plant operator, and not by the\nNRC. This clarified the need for this legislation.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nSimilar to A.8746 of 2017/2018\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nThere are no fiscal implications to the State in association with the\npassing of this legislation, however, there could be benefits for the\nlocal assessing authority that hosts a non-operational facility where\nspent fuel rods are stored.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
